## Why This Is Asked
Auditing and non-repudiation in CNF gateways are critical for multi-tenant environments. This question validates practical expertise with Kubernetes Secrets, HMAC signing, sidecar log pipelines, and retention configuration—essential skills for implementing post-deployment security controls.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped auditing and non-repudiation
- HMAC signing using per-tenant Kubernetes Secrets
- Sidecar-based log forwarding to in-cluster sinks
- ConfigMap-driven log retention and namespace scoping

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signLog(logLine, secret) {
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(logLine)
    .digest('hex');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a comprehensive cross-architecture supply chain security and runtime trust workflow that addresses edge deployment challenges, including offline scenarios and real-time policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-architecture image signing and SBOM provenance tracking
- Runtime attestation with architecture-aware claim verification
- Policy enforcement using OPA Gatekeeper with architecture-specific constraints
- Edge connectivity management and canary-driven rollback mechanisms

## Code Example
```javascript
function isAllowed(image, arch) {
  // Verify Cosign signature for specific architecture
  const signatureValid = verifyCosignSignature(image, arch);
  const sbomPresent = checkSBOMPresence(image, arch);
  const attestationValid = verifyInTotoAttestation(image, arch);

  return signatureValid && sbomPresent && attestationValid;
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant mTLS, dynamic certificate reloading, and sidecar patterns. It tests knowledge of secure-by-default design and how certificate rotation integrates with live production traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant TLS materials and certificate management
- Sidecar dynamic TLS reload mechanisms
- Internal CA API integration and certificate rotation
- Header-based tenant identification (X-TENANT-ID)
- Gateway validation rules and trust models

## Code Example
```go
// Fetch tenant certificate, cache with TTL, and wire into TLS config
func (s *Sidecar) getClientCert(tenantID string) (*tls.Certificate, error) {
    if cert, ok := s.certCache.Get(tenantID); ok {
        return cert, nil
    }
    cert, err := s.caClient.FetchCertificate(tenantID)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    s.certCache.Set(tenantID, cert, certTTL)
    return cert, nil
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

